WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.475
What changed at FedRAMP?

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:02.513
Three things.

3
00:00:02.513 --> 00:00:05.950
First, services are now
certified, in Classes A to D.

4
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.818
FedRAMP's February 2026 notice says Class
C includes the current Moderate baseline

5
00:00:11.818 --> 00:00:15.255
and Class D the current
High, and that agencies may

6
00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:19.363
authorize at any security
category they deem appropriate.

7
00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:23.051
Second, the rules have no
provider authorization boundary.

8
00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:26.728
In the machine-readable ruleset,

9
00:00:26.728 --> 00:00:32.450
version 2026.10.13.01, that
phrase occurs zero times.

10
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:37.208
The word boundary appears eight times,
four of them as a tag on rule families:

11
00:00:37.208 --> 00:00:40.584
minimum assessment scope,
cryptographic module use,

12
00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:45.188
vulnerability detection and response,
and certification data sharing.

13
00:00:45.188 --> 00:00:48.300
Security category may vary by resource.

14
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.750
Third, change is notified, not re-approved,
and the deadlines are in business days.

15
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.462
Routine recurring changes need no notice.

16
00:00:56.462 --> 00:01:00.187
Adaptive changes, within 10
business days after finishing.

17
00:01:00.187 --> 00:01:04.626
Transformative changes, initial
plans 30 business days before.

18
00:01:04.626 --> 00:01:08.806
Adoption is mandatory on January 1, 2027,

19
00:01:08.806 --> 00:01:13.180
and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.
